<h2>The disciplinary case</h2>
<p>The appellant joined Periyar University as Assistant Registrar in May 2000 and rose to Deputy Registrar. Following a complaint about his assets, a preliminary inquiry and then a One-man Commission examined a set of charges. Three were held proved: that he influenced the grant of affiliation to a college run by his wife; that a house standing in his mother&rsquo;s name was really his acquisition; and that he had purchased a car beyond his known sources of income. The Syndicate accepted the report and removed him from service with effect from 27 July 2013.</p>
<p>A learned Single Judge allowed his writ petition, finding the conclusions perverse because they rested on no evidence — the affiliation charge, for instance, was held proved on the reasoning that his influence &ldquo;could not be ruled out&rdquo;. In the Letters Patent appeal, a Division Bench reversed the Single Judge in 2019 and restored the removal. The Supreme Court has now restored the writ court&rsquo;s order.</p>

<h2>The appellate discipline the judgment enforces</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old">
    <h4>What the Division Bench did</h4>
    <p>Re-appreciated the material, drew adverse inferences from the employee&rsquo;s failure to produce title documents, and substituted its own assessment for the writ court&rsquo;s on charges the inquiry had supported with surmise.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">&rarr;</div>
  <div class="col new">
    <h4>What an LPA bench may do</h4>
    <p>Correct a writ-court decision only where its findings are demonstrably erroneous — a glaring mistake apparent on the record. A decision that is otherwise just and reasonable stands, even if some error of fact or law can be pointed out.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2>Three strands of reasoning</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h4>No evidence means perversity</h4><p>A finding unsupported by any material is perverse, and perversity is a recognised ground for certiorari. Suspicion, however strong, and inferences built on &ldquo;cannot be ruled out&rdquo; reasoning are not evidence.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Discretion deserves deference</h4><p>The writ court&rsquo;s discretionary assessment commands restraint from the appellate bench. The LPA exists to correct demonstrable error, not to offer the losing side a fresh round of fact-finding.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Alternative remedy is no absolute bar</h4><p>Exhaustion of statutory remedies is a self-imposed rule of convenience. Where removal from service visits severe consequences, the writ court may act directly.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Relevance for Delhi practice</h2>
<p>In the Delhi High Court, intra-court appeals from writ-court judgments travel as LPAs, and the standard articulated here will be the touchstone: an appellant must identify a demonstrable, glaring error, not merely argue the evidence afresh. The decision pairs naturally with the same week&rsquo;s ruling in <em>Sanjay Prasad v. Madhusudan Dwivedi</em>, which examines when an intra-court appeal is maintainable at all; together they map both the gate and the ground of writ appeals.</p>
<div class="check">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>For appellants:</strong> frame the challenge around perversity, jurisdictional error or a glaring misreading of the record — a &ldquo;different possible view&rdquo; argument is designed to fail.</li>
    <li><strong>For respondents:</strong> defend the writ court&rsquo;s discretion as such; the appellate bench need not agree with every line of reasoning to affirm the result.</li>
    <li><strong>For disciplinary authorities:</strong> inquiry findings must rest on legal evidence. Conclusions phrased as possibilities invite certiorari, and a removal built on them may not survive.</li>
  </ul>
</div>
<div class="note"><p>This article is for general information only and is not legal advice. The judgment should be read in full before being relied upon in any proceeding.</p></div>
